2. ''Thermoelectric Power, Series, Inversion.''—The limiting value, <math>dE/dt</math>, of the coefficient, <math>p</math>, for an infinitesimal difference, <math>dt</math>, between the junctions is called the ''Thermoelectric Power'' of the couple. One metal (A) is said to be thermoelectrically positive to another (B), if positive electricity flows from A to B across the cold junction when the circuit is completed. The opposite convention is sometimes adopted, but the above is the most convenient in practice, as the circuit is generally broken at or near the cold junction for the insertion of the galvanometer. Seebeck found that the metals could be arranged in a ''Thermoelectric Series'', in the order of their power when combined with any one metal, such that the power of any thermocouple <math>p</math>, composed of the metals A and B, was equal to the algebraic difference <math>(p'-p'')</math> of their powers when combined with the standard metal C. The order of the metals in this

